WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Book 1 computed four returns for Orontes in 2025: a return on assets of 10.22 per cent, a return on equity of 16.15 per cent, an earnings yield of 3.96 per cent and a shareholder return of 19.30 per cent. All four were returns on a year that had already happened. This chapter keeps the arithmetic and changes the tense.
1.1  Rates of return
A rate of return is what was earned, over what was put in, over a stated period. The three parts of that sentence are where the marks are.
The holding period return is the simplest and the one the exam uses most. It is the income received plus the change in value, over the value at the start. For an Orontes shareholder in 2025: the price rose 9.25 − 7.80 = 1.45 and the dividend was 0.0556, so the return is 1.45 + 0.0556 = 1.5056 on an opening 7.80, which is 1.5056 ÷ 7.80 = 19.30 per cent.
Where the period is not a year, the return has to be annualised before it can be compared with anything. A return of 19.30 per cent over two years is not 19.30 per cent a year; compounding makes it 1.1930 raised to the power of one half, which is 9.22 per cent a year. Where the period is shorter than a year the same relationship runs the other way: 2 per cent over a quarter compounds to 8.24 per cent a year, not 8.
Averaging a series of returns has the same trap in a sharper form. The arithmetic mean of +40 per cent and −30 per cent is 5 per cent, and an investor who lived through those two years has lost money: 1.40 × 0.70 = 0.98, so one dollar became 98 cents and the true compound return is about minus 1 per cent a year. The geometric mean is the honest figure for a series of returns actually earned; the arithmetic mean is the right figure for a single period drawn at random from the same distribution, which is why both appear in the exam and why the question always says which.
Figure F601-02 sets out the four.
Figure F601-02. Four rates of return, and when each is right
@table
Measure | Computation | Use it for
Holding period return | Income plus price change, over opening value | One period, any length
Annualised return | The holding period return compounded to a year | Comparing periods of different lengths
Arithmetic mean | The simple average of the periods | One period drawn from the same distribution
Geometric mean | The compound rate that links start to finish | What an investor actually earned
@end
Finally, a return quoted in money of the day is a nominal return, and what it buys depends on inflation. The real return divides one by the other: at 19.30 per cent nominal and 4 per cent inflation, 1.1930 ÷ 1.04 = 1.1471, so the real return is 14.71 per cent. The approximation that every candidate reaches for, 19.30 − 4.00 = 15.30 per cent, is close enough at these rates and wrong by a percentage point when rates are high — which is exactly when the question asks.
EXAM TRAP   Say which period and which basis
A return with no period attached is meaningless. Annualise before comparing.
Geometric for a series lived through, arithmetic for a single expected period. The question will tell you; the figures will not.
Nominal less inflation is an approximation. Where the question gives both rates to one decimal, use the division.
