1.2  Expected return, and the spread around it
A return that has happened is a fact. A return that has not happened yet is a distribution, and the exam describes it with a short list of outcomes and their probabilities.
The expected return is the probability-weighted average of the outcomes. Consider a project whose return is 25 per cent if the harvest is good, 10 per cent if it is ordinary and minus 5 per cent if it fails, with probabilities of 0.2, 0.5 and 0.3. Then 0.2 × 25 = 5.0, 0.5 × 10 = 5.0 and 0.3 × 5 = 1.5 in the other direction, so the expected return is 5.0 + 5.0 − 1.5 = 8.5 per cent.
Nobody earns 8.5 per cent. The expected return is the centre of the distribution and not a forecast of any single year, which is why it must always be quoted with a measure of how wide the distribution is.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the spread, and what it is worth knowing
Step 1. The deviations. Each outcome's distance from the 8.5 per cent expectation: 25.0 − 8.5 = 16.5, then 10.0 − 8.5 = 1.5, then an outcome of minus 5 which is 13.5 below the mean.
Step 2. The variance. Square each deviation and weight it by its probability: 0.2 × 272.25 = 54.45, 0.5 × 2.25 = 1.125 and 0.3 × 182.25 = 54.675. The three add to 110.25.
Step 3. The standard deviation and the coefficient of variation. The square root of 110.25 is 10.5 percentage points. Expressed against the expected return, the coefficient of variation is 10.5 ÷ 8.5 = 1.24.
Check. Two projects with the same expected return are not the same project. The standard deviation says how far a single year is likely to fall from the centre; the coefficient of variation scales that spread by the size of the return, which is what lets a small project be compared with a large one. The exam asks for the coefficient whenever the two projects differ in size, and for the standard deviation whenever they do not.
Figure F601-03 lays out the computation.
Figure F601-03. The expected return and its spread
@table
Outcome | Probability | Return | Weighted return | Weighted squared deviation
Good harvest | 0.2 | 25.0% | 5.0 | 54.45
Ordinary | 0.5 | 10.0% | 5.0 | 1.125
Failure | 0.3 | (5.0)% | (1.5) | 54.675
Total | 1.0 | | 8.5% | 110.25
@end
