1.3  Systematic and unsystematic risk
Not all risk is the same kind, and the difference decides whether an investor is paid for carrying it.
Unsystematic risk, also called company-specific or diversifiable risk, belongs to one company or one industry. A fire in Orontes's plant, a recall of one product, the loss of its largest customer, a strike, a lawsuit, the departure of a chief executive. These events are independent across companies: a fire at Orontes tells you nothing about a fire at a glass works in Amman.
Systematic risk, also called market risk or non-diversifiable risk, belongs to the economy. A rise in interest rates, a recession, a tax change, a war, a currency crisis. Every company is affected, in different degrees but in the same direction.
The consequence is the most important sentence in Section B. Because unsystematic risk is independent across companies, holding many companies at once makes most of it disappear: the fire at one is offset by the good year at another. Systematic risk cannot be removed by holding more companies, because it moves them all together. So an investor who can diversify will not pay to avoid unsystematic risk — and will not be paid for bearing it. Only systematic risk carries a reward. Chapter 2 turns that sentence into a formula.
A company's management sees it differently, and the exam tests the difference. A diversified shareholder does not care about the fire; the chief executive, the workforce and the lender very much do, because they cannot diversify their exposure to this one company. That is why companies buy insurance and hedge currencies when, on a strict reading of portfolio theory, their shareholders need neither.
Figure F601-04 contrasts them.
Figure F601-04. The two kinds of risk
@table
| Unsystematic risk | Systematic risk
Also called | Company-specific, diversifiable | Market risk, non-diversifiable
Examples | Fire, recall, strike, lawsuit, a lost customer | Recession, rates, inflation, tax, war
Removable by diversification? | Yes, almost entirely | No
Rewarded by the market? | No | Yes
Who still cares | Managers, employees, lenders | Everybody
@end
EXAM TRAP   Diversifiable means unpaid
A risk that can be diversified away earns no premium, however frightening it is to the company carrying it.
Diversification reduces unsystematic risk; it does not eliminate risk. A portfolio of a hundred shares still falls in a recession.
The distinction is about the source of the risk, not its size. A large company-specific risk is still unsystematic.
