1.5  Risk and return together
The relationship between risk and return is not a tendency observed in the data. It is a condition for a market to clear. If two assets offered the same return and one was riskier, nobody would hold the riskier one, and its price would fall until its return rose enough to attract a buyer. Risk and return move together because prices make them.
The structure that follows is simple and is used in every chapter of this book. A required return is a risk-free rate plus a premium for the risk carried.
The risk-free rate is what a lender earns with no credit risk and no reinvestment uncertainty over the period — in practice a government security of matching maturity. It compensates for two things only: waiting, and expected inflation.
The risk premium is everything else, and it is specific to the asset. A corporate bond pays more than a treasury because the company may default. A share pays more than the company's own bond because the shareholder is paid last. A small company pays more than a large one, and an illiquid asset more than a liquid one.
Orontes's own statements show the ladder. The company borrows from Levant Commerce Bank at 6 per cent. Book 1 charged its shareholders 10 per cent as a required return when it computed economic profit, and the shareholders actually earned 19.30 per cent in 2025. The gap between 6 and 10 is the price of standing behind the lender; the gap between 10 and 19.30 is the year turning out well.
Figure F601-06 draws the ladder.
Figure F601-06. The required-return ladder
@table
Claim | Required return | Why it sits there
Government security | 3% to 4% | Waiting and expected inflation only
Senior secured bank debt | 6% | Default risk, with security and covenants
Unsecured corporate bond | 7% to 8% | Default risk, no security
Preferred stock | 9% | Paid after debt; dividend not contractual
Common equity | 10% and above | Paid last, and only what is left
@end
Two consequences close the chapter, and both are examined.
The first is that a company's cost of capital is its investors' required return seen from the other side. Every rate in that ladder is a cost to Orontes and a return to somebody. Chapter 8 builds the weighted average of them.
The second is that risk aversion is a matter of degree, not of principle. A risk-averse investor is not one who refuses risk; it is one who requires compensation for it. An investor who required no compensation would be risk-neutral, and an investor who paid for risk would be risk-seeking — which is a fair description of a lottery ticket and of nothing a company should be doing with its shareholders' money.
