LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Risk"
English risk in finance is neutral: it means the dispersion of possible outcomes, good and bad alike, and an asset whose return might be far above expectations is risky in exactly the sense meant here. Everyday English uses the word only for the bad half, and that is the reading candidates import by mistake. Arabic مخاطرة carries the same everyday bias towards danger, and the technical sense has to be learned. French distinguishes helpfully: risque for the dispersion, aléa for the chance element, and danger for what everyday English means. Note the phrase risk-averse: it does not mean refusing risk, it means requiring payment for it, and a candidate who reads it as refusal will answer half the questions in this book backwards.
