Chapter summary
Figure F601-08 brings the chapter together.
Figure F601-08. Chapter 1 summary map: the key rule for each section.
A rate of return is income plus the change in value over the opening value, which made an Orontes shareholder 19.30 per cent in 2025; it must be annualised before it is compared, averaged geometrically when it is a series lived through, and divided by one plus inflation to become a real return of 14.71 per cent. A return still to come is a distribution: the probability-weighted expectation of 8.5 per cent has a standard deviation of 10.5 points around it and a coefficient of variation of 1.24, and two projects with the same expectation are not the same project. Risk comes in two kinds, and only one is paid for: company-specific risk disappears in a portfolio and earns nothing, while market risk moves every company together and carries the whole of the premium — though a manager who cannot diversify still hedges what a shareholder would not pay to remove. Four risks are named by the syllabus and all four are visible in Orontes's statements: credit in the allowance of $156,000, currency in the imported sesame, interest rate in the $6,000,000 of debt and market in the $42,000 of trading securities. And every required return is a risk-free rate plus a premium, which is why Orontes's bank takes 6 per cent and its shareholders require 10.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P1-01  A share bought at 20.00 pays a dividend of 0.80 and is sold at 23.00. The holding period return is:
A.	19.00 per cent
B.	15.00 per cent
C.	4.00 per cent
D.	13.04 per cent
P1-02  Returns of +50 per cent and then −40 per cent leave an investor with:
A.	a gain of 5 per cent a year.
B.	0.90 of each dollar invested.
C.	1.10 of each dollar invested.
D.	exactly what was invested.
P1-03  Nominal return 12.0 per cent, inflation 5.0 per cent. The real return is closest to:
A.	6.67 per cent
B.	7.00 per cent
C.	17.00 per cent
D.	2.40 per cent
P1-04  The expected return of 30 per cent at 0.3, 10 per cent at 0.4 and minus 10 per cent at 0.3 is:
A.	10.0 per cent
B.	13.0 per cent
C.	30.0 per cent
D.	3.0 per cent
P1-05  Which is unsystematic risk?
A.	A recession
B.	A strike at one company's factory
C.	A rise in the central bank's rate
D.	A change in the corporate tax rate
P1-06  Diversification removes:
A.	most unsystematic risk and no systematic risk.
B.	all risk.
C.	systematic risk only.
D.	neither kind.
P1-07  A company's receivables carry:
A.	credit risk.
B.	interest rate risk.
C.	foreign exchange translation risk.
D.	no risk, because they are current assets.
P1-08  Translation exposure arises from:
A.	consolidating the statements of a foreign subsidiary.
B.	a contract to buy goods in a foreign currency.
C.	the long-run competitiveness of the business.
D.	a domestic bank loan.
P1-09  The coefficient of variation is used in preference to the standard deviation when:
A.	the two investments differ in size or expected return.
B.	the returns are equal.
C.	the risk is systematic.
D.	the period is longer than a year.
P1-10  An investor who requires no compensation for bearing risk is:
A.	risk-neutral.
B.	risk-averse.
C.	risk-seeking.
D.	diversified.
Case-style practice: two projects for Orontes
Orontes can invest $2,000,000 in one of two projects. Project Roasting has an expected return of 12 per cent with a standard deviation of 4 points. Project Export has an expected return of 18 per cent with a standard deviation of 15 points. The company's required return on its equity is 10 per cent. The chief executive says the second project is obviously better because it earns more.
Required. Compute the coefficient of variation for each. Say which you would recommend and why, naming the kind of risk the export project adds and whether Orontes's shareholders are compensated for it. Under 250 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC1-1  Answer A. Income plus the change in value, over what was paid at the start.
B is wrong. Dividing by the closing value answers a different question.
C is wrong. Ignoring the income understates the return.
D is wrong. A coupon rate is a contractual rate, not a realised return.
SC1-2  Answer B. 1.40 × 0.70 = 0.98, so a dollar became 98 cents.
A is wrong. That is the arithmetic mean, which nobody earned.
C is wrong. The investor is slightly worse off.
D is wrong. The two returns do not add.
SC1-3  Answer A. 1.1930 ÷ 1.04 = 1.1471, a real return of 14.71 per cent.
B is wrong. Inflation is divided out, not added.
C is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
D is wrong. That is the nominal return.
SC1-4  Answer A. Each outcome weighted by its probability.
B is wrong. The best case is one outcome among several.
C is wrong. The expectation is the centre, not a forecast of one year.
D is wrong. Ignoring the middle outcome and the probabilities gives a different figure.
SC1-5  Answer A. Spread divided by expectation.
B is wrong. That inverts the ratio.
C is wrong. The variance is in squared units.
D is wrong. The square of the standard deviation is the variance.
SC1-6  Answer A. With equal expectations, less spread is preferred.
B is wrong. That describes a risk-seeker.
C is wrong. Equal returns make the risk the deciding factor.
D is wrong. Higher variance is the same as higher standard deviation.
SC1-7  Answer A. A fire at one plant is independent of other companies.
B is wrong. Systematic risk moves every company together.
C is wrong. Credit risk is about a counterparty's failure to pay.
D is wrong. It is a risk, and an insurable one.
SC1-8  Answer A. Only non-diversifiable risk is rewarded.
B is wrong. Unsystematic risk can be diversified away for nothing.
C is wrong. The two are not treated alike.
D is wrong. Systematic risk carries the whole premium.
SC1-9  Answer A. The manager's human capital and reputation sit in one company.
B is wrong. Both parties are reasoning correctly from different positions.
C is wrong. Currency risk has both systematic and company-specific elements.
D is wrong. Hedging has a cost, which is part of the point.
SC1-10  Answer A. The allowance estimates the receivables that will not be collected.
B is wrong. Interest rate risk concerns the price of money.
C is wrong. The receivable here is in the company's own currency.
D is wrong. Market risk concerns traded prices.
SC1-11  Answer A. A contract already struck in a foreign currency is transaction exposure.
B is wrong. Translation exposure arises on consolidating a foreign subsidiary.
C is wrong. The exposure is real and measurable.
D is wrong. The risk here is the rate, not the counterparty.
SC1-12  Answer A. A risk-free rate plus a premium for the risk.
B is wrong. The realised return is what happened, not what was required.
C is wrong. Inflation is one component of the risk-free rate.
D is wrong. A government coupon is close to the risk-free rate, not to a required return on a risky asset.
SC1-13  Answer A. Aversion means requiring payment, not refusing.
B is wrong. An investor who refused all risk would hold nothing but treasuries, which is a corner case and not the definition.
C is wrong. Indifference is risk-neutrality.
D is wrong. Paying for risk is risk-seeking.
SC1-14  Answer A. One side's cost is the other side's required return.
B is wrong. Bank debt is one component of several.
C is wrong. The return on assets is a historical result.
D is wrong. The cost of capital rises with the risk of the business.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Holding period return | 19.00 per cent: 23.00 − 20.00 = 3.00 of price gain, plus 0.80 of dividend, and 3.80 ÷ 20.00 = 19.00 per cent.
Real return | 6.67 per cent: 1.1200 ÷ 1.05 = 1.0667.
Geometric mean of +50% then −40% | A loss: 1.50 × 0.60 = 0.90, so one dollar became 90 cents, about minus 5.1 per cent a year.
Expected return | 10.0 per cent: 0.3 × 30 = 9.0, 0.4 × 10 = 4.0, and 0.3 × 10 = 3.0 in the other direction, so 9.0 + 4.0 − 3.0 = 10.0.
Standard deviation | 15.49 points: the weighted squared deviations are 0.3 × 400 = 120, nil for the middle outcome, and 0.3 × 400 = 120, giving a variance of 240.
Coefficient of variation | 1.549: 15.49 ÷ 10.0.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P1-01  Answer A. 3.80 ÷ 20.00 = 19.00 per cent.
B is wrong. This omits the dividend.
C is wrong. This is the dividend yield on cost.
D is wrong. This divides by the selling price.
P1-02  Answer B. 1.50 × 0.60 = 0.90.
A is wrong. That is the arithmetic mean, which was not earned.
C is wrong. The order of the two returns does not create a gain.
D is wrong. The investor is 10 per cent worse off.
P1-03  Answer A. 1.1200 ÷ 1.05 = 1.0667.
B is wrong. That is the subtraction approximation, which is close and not exact.
C is wrong. Inflation is divided out, not added.
D is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
P1-04  Answer A. 9.0 + 4.0 − 3.0 = 10.0 per cent.
B is wrong. This ignores the negative outcome's sign.
C is wrong. That is the best case alone.
D is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
P1-05  Answer B. A strike at one factory affects one company.
A is wrong. A recession is systematic.
C is wrong. A policy rate is systematic.
D is wrong. A tax change affects every company.
P1-06  Answer A. Company-specific risks offset each other; market risk does not.
B is wrong. No portfolio of shares is riskless.
C is wrong. Systematic risk is precisely what cannot be diversified.
D is wrong. Diversification works on unsystematic risk.
P1-07  Answer A. A receivable is a promise by a customer to pay.
B is wrong. Interest rate risk concerns the price of money.
C is wrong. Translation risk concerns foreign subsidiaries.
D is wrong. Being current does not make a receivable certain.
P1-08  Answer A. Translation exposure arises on consolidation.
B is wrong. That is transaction exposure.
C is wrong. That is economic exposure.
D is wrong. A domestic loan carries no currency exposure.
P1-09  Answer A. Scaling the spread by the return allows unlike investments to be compared.
B is wrong. With equal returns the standard deviations can be compared directly.
C is wrong. The coefficient is not about the source of the risk.
D is wrong. The period does not determine which measure is used.
P1-10  Answer A. Risk-neutral means indifferent to risk.
B is wrong. A risk-averse investor requires compensation.
C is wrong. A risk-seeker pays for risk.
D is wrong. Diversification is a strategy, not an attitude to risk.
Case-style set: two projects for Orontes
The coefficients of variation. Project Roasting: 4 ÷ 12 = 0.33. Project Export: 15 ÷ 18 = 0.83. For each point of expected return, the export project carries about two and a half times the spread.
The recommendation. I would take Project Roasting on these figures. Both clear the 10 per cent the shareholders require, so the question is not whether either is worth doing but which buys its return more cheaply in risk, and the roasting project does so by a wide margin. The export project's extra six points of expected return are bought with eleven extra points of standard deviation.
The kind of risk. Most of what the export project adds is foreign exchange risk — transaction exposure on each contract, and economic exposure if a sustained move in the rate makes Orontes uncompetitive in the new market. Some of that is systematic, because currency moves with the economy, and some is specific to one trade route and one customer base.
Whether shareholders are compensated. Only for the systematic part. The company-specific element of the export risk earns nothing in an efficient market, because a diversified shareholder has already removed it by holding other companies. That is the argument the chief executive is missing: a higher expected return is not by itself a better project, and a return bought with diversifiable risk is bought with risk nobody will pay for.
A fuller answer would ask what the two projects do to each other. If the export project's returns move against the domestic business, the combination could be less risky than either alone, which is the subject of Chapter 2.
